*.html text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf

# Published artifacts must check out byte-identical to their blobs.
# keld-benches runs core.autocrlf=true, and result documents and schema files
# were covered by no attribute at all, so a Windows clone rewrote them to CRLF
# and any byte-level claim about a published document stopped holding for
# anyone who was not its author. Same failure the fixture .gitattributes
# documents, on the artifacts the repository exists to publish.
#
# Path-scoped, not *.json / *.md: windows/electron/hello/node_modules is
# committed, and a global rule would renormalize vendored files.
windows/bench/results/** text eol=lf
schema/**                text eol=lf
windows/bench/windows-first-paint*.json text eol=lf

# Hello fixtures. Their entire value is being BYTE-IDENTICAL to `keld create
# hello` output at the pinned Keld SHA, and `keld create` writes LF.
#
# keld-benches runs core.autocrlf=true, so without an attribute a Windows
# checkout rewrites these to CRLF and they stop matching byte-for-byte: the
# blob stays LF, the working tree does not. Not hypothetical -- every file
# under macos/keld/hello checked out CRLF, index.html at 366 bytes against its
# own 349-byte blob, so that fixture's identity claim was unverifiable to
# anyone cloning on Windows.
#
# This rule covers every OS fixture dir. It replaces a per-directory
# .gitattributes that pinned only the Windows fixture while its own comment
# cited the broken macOS one as evidence -- the exact drift a second copy
# produces.
*/keld/hello/** text eol=lf
